 is a standard resilience pattern, the aggregate effect of a 420 percent increase in retry traffic directed at an already rate-limited endpoint created a negative feedback loop. Each retry consumed a portion of the available rate limit budget, reducing the capacity for legitimate first-attempt requests to succeed and generating further 429 responses.\n\nThe decision to scale workers from 8 to 16 at 09:20 produced only a 6 percent reduction in queue depth over ten minutes. This result is diagnostic: if the bottleneck were processing capacity, doubling workers should have approximately doubled throughput. Instead, the additional workers generated more requests to dependency B, which responded with more 429 errors, effectively neutralizing the benefit of parallelism. This observation confirms that the constraint was the external rate limit, not internal processing capacity.\n\nAt 09:26, temporarily disabling retries caused queue depth to fall 38 percent in six minutes. This intervention removed the amplified retry traffic from the request stream, allowing first-attempt requests to succeed within the available rate limit budget. The concurrent increase in direct failure rate was an expected consequence, as tasks that would have been retried were instead immediately marked as failed. The net effect on queue health was strongly positive, confirming that retries were the dominant amplifier of the incident.\n\nThe definitive resolution occurred at 09:35 when dependency B rate limits were raised. HTTP 429 responses fell below 1 percent, and queue depth returned below 150 within eleven minutes. No other variable changed during this window, and the speed and completeness of the recovery confirm that rate limiting by dependency B was the primary sustaining cause of the incident.\n\nAn alternative hypothesis attributes the incident entirely to a sudden traffic spike without dependency B rate limiting being the primary driver. This hypothesis is less supported because doubling worker capacity did not meaningfully reduce queue depth, and recovery correlated precisely with the rate limit increase rather than any change in inbound traffic volume. A pure traffic spike would have been mitigated by horizontal scaling. A second alternative posits a latent software defect causing tasks to hang. This is less supported because latency increased to a finite value of 14.2 seconds rather than becoming unbounded, and the system recovered fully once dependency B was unblocked. A third alternative suggests network degradation between workers and dependency B. This is less supported because the failure mode was HTTP 429, a deliberate server-side rate limit response, not connection timeouts or transport-layer errors.\n\nThe retry policy requires revision. Exponential backoff with a maximum of three retries was insufficient to prevent amplification under the observed conditions. An adaptive policy that detects 429 response rates and reduces retry aggressiveness proportionally would prevent similar feedback loops. Circuit breaker patterns should be implemented to halt requests to dependency B when error rates exceed a defined threshold, allowing the dependency to recover before resuming traffic.\n\nThe staged remediation plan should proceed in three phases. The immediate phase should deploy circuit breakers and adaptive retry policies for dependency B. The medium-term phase should establish queue depth alerting, negotiate higher baseline rate limits, and implement bulkhead isolation to prevent dependency B failures from affecting other service paths. The long-term phase should evaluate architectural decoupling through asynchronous message processing, reducing synchronous dependency on B and providing natural backpressure during load spikes.\n\nEdge cases warrant attention in the remediation plan. If dependency B returns partial or degraded responses rather than explicit 429 errors, circuit breakers based solely on HTTP status codes may fail to detect the degradation. Monitoring should include response time percentiles and payload completeness checks. If multiple dependencies experience rate limiting simultaneously, bulkhead isolation becomes critical to prevent a single dependency failure from consuming all available worker capacity. Auto-scaling policies that increase worker count during queue buildup should incorporate dependency health checks to avoid generating additional failed requests. Retry jitter, while useful for preventing thundering herd effects, may be insufficient when the underlying rate limit budget is fundamentally inadequate for the request volume.",
